// == The Beatles ==

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: SLAM Example::John
**
**  Paragraphs are easy, simply leave an empty line between your
**  text and it'll be interpreted as a new paragraph. Like so.
**
**  And here's a line break using \<br>. <br>Like so.
**
**  Simple formatting can be achieved by surrounding the text in differing
**  number of quotes. ''Italics'', i.e. \'\'Italics\'\' is 2 quotes. '''Bold''',
**  i.e. \'\'\'Bold\'\'\' is three quotes. Unsurprisingly, '''''Bold Italic''''',
**  i.e. \'\'\'\'\'Bold Italic\'\'\'\'\' is 5 quotes. Alternatively,
**  <tt>monospace</tt> text can be achieved using \<tt>. Note that we can
**  actually print markup characters by using the '''\''' escape character.
**  Strikethrough is <strike>not supported at this time</strike> now
**  supported via \<strike>.
**
**  A multi-line block of text that is preformatted and needs to
**  remain so can be enclosed in \<pre\>. For example:
**
**  <pre>
**  ////\\     //||\\     //\|\\      ///||\
**  /`O-O'     ` @ @\     //o o//       a a
**     ]          >        ) | (         _)
**     -          -          -           ~
**   John       Paul       George      Ringo
**  </pre>
**
**  Bulleted lists are lines starting with *. The number of *'s determine
**  the level:
**
**  * I am the eggman
**  ** they are the eggmen
**  * I am the walrus
**  ** goo goo g'joob !
**
**  And they terminate with the next non-bulleted paragraph. Numbered
**  lists are identical, except they use a #:
**
**  # Semolina pilchard
**  ## climbing up
**  ## the Eiffel tower
**  # Elementary penguin
**  ## singing
**  ## hari
**  ## krishna
**  # Man, you should
**  ## have seen
**  ## them kicking
**  ### Edgar
**  #### Allan
**  ##### Poe
**
**  Finally, we can implement a definition list by starting a line
**  with a semi-colon (;) and delimiting the term and description using a
**  colon (:) like so :
**
**  ; John : Chap with glasses, always seems melancholy
**  ; Paul : Cheery chap, likes a sing-song
**  ; George : Quiet chap, likes odd music, plays a mean guitar
**  ; Ringo : Mmm, funny looking chap banging drums at the back
**
**  Headings are also available within a description block. They apply
**  to this description block only and can be two levels deep. A first-level
**  heading uses \~\~1st Level\~\~ (i.e. 2 tildes), and a second-level
**  uses \~\~\~2nd Level\~\~\~ (3 tildes). Here's a first- and second-level header:
**
**  ~~First-Level Header~~
**  This is a first-level header.
**
**  ~~~Second-Level Header~~~
**  This is a second-level header.
**
**  ~~Another First-Level Header~~
**  This is another first-level header. Exciting, no ?
**
****************************************************************/

SLAM Example::John = Func(
    Double( Strawberry ),   // comments here will associate with the strawberry argument. I prefer raspberries
    /*
    **  You can put '''stand-alone''' comments here. These will be associated with the
    **  following argument, in this case <tt>Fields</tt>. If you put in a stand-alone
    **  comment '''and''' a single line comment after the argument, the single-line will
    **  take precedence and be placed at the top, then this, separated by a paragraph.
    */
    Structure( Fields ),    // and this will associate with the fields argument. Are all fields green ?
)
Returns( String(), Error() )
{   // note we don't really pay attention to anything in here, hooray !
    Return( String( Strawberry ) + String( Fields ) + "Forever" );
};

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: SLAM Example::Paul
**
**  We can refer to another identifier (currently a function or
**  typed structure) by wrapping in double brackets and
**  commencing with an @. For instance <tt>[[@SLAM Example::John]]</tt>
**  results in a link to [[@SLAM Example::John]]. This can be in the
**  same script like the previous example, or in any other script, like
**  [[@Archive::DiddleMktData]] or [[@DateFns::MonthName]].
**  It can also be a built-in function like [[@TdsInMemory]].
**
**  You can name the link by placing the name after a pipe. So if you
**  want to refer to a function with alternate text,
**  [[@SLAM Example::John|you would insert markup inside the brackets]].
**
**  If the identifier hasn't been slammed or doesn't exist, it'll appear monospaced,
**  like [[@Clearly::NonSense]].
****************************************************************/

SLAM Example::Paul = Func(
    String( Yesterday ) = "Sad Song" // well, I think it is anyway
)
Returns( Array() )
{   // blah-blah-blah, you won't see me !
    Return( [ Yesterday, [ "All", "Troubles", "Far", "Away" ] ] );
};

