/****************************************************************
**  Routine: SLAM Example::Robert
**
**  External links can be specified very easily by surrounding the URL in
**  \[ and \]. For example, search away with [http://www.google.com].
**
**  If the URL is very long and ugly, you can place a space after the url
**  and then put anchor text, e.g. \[url anchor text\]. This is OK
**  because spaces aren't part of URLs (just slang variables!). So
**  [http://home.gs.com/gsweb/gsc GS Home Page] is much nicer
**  than [http://home.gs.com/gsweb/gsc].
**
**  At this point, anchor text is NOT processed by our markup processor unlike on Wikipedia.
**  So if you specify \[http://www.google.com \'\'\'anchor text\'\'\'\], it will simply show up as
**  [http://www.google.com '''anchor text''']. Use explicit HTML tags such as \<b\>, like this:
**  \[http://www.google.com My anchor text with \<b\>bolded words\<\/b\>\] which yields:
**  [http://www.google.com My anchor text with <b>bolded words</b>].
**
**  You can specify a relative url using URL: prefix, like \[URL:foo/bar.html this\]
**  (which generates [URL:foo/bar.html this]). Not so useful here, but more useful
**  in FAQs.
**
**  And email distributions needn't feel left out in the link-fest, you
**  can insert an email link like <tt>[URL:mailto:EL{gs-ep-dpg-slangdev}]</tt>
**  or <tt>[URL:mailto:EL{gs-ep-dpg-slangdev} Cod Rights]</tt> which
**  yields [URL:mailto:EL{gs-ep-dpg-slangdev}] and
**  [URL:mailto:EL{gs-ep-dpg-slangdev} Cod Rights] respectively.
**  The <tt>EL{...}</tt> syntax is resolved to get the correct full address.
**  But you may also want to consider using the simpler metadir keyword,
**  like this: [[metadir:gs-secdb-symbols-dev]].
**
**  You can include creepy heads with <tt>[[Yams:goodfj]]</tt> which renders as [[Yams:goodfj]], or
**  just <tt>[[y:walkerpa]]</tt> which just gives [[y:walkerpa]].
**
**  You can link to dirget for individuals, mailing lists or department codes
**  like this: <tt>[[metadir:walkerpa Paul]]</tt> (which gives [[metadir:walkerpa Paul]]).
**  Another example is [[metadir:F581 core users]].
**  You can also link to an email distro like this: <tt>[[metadir:gs-secdb-symbols-dev]]</tt>, which generates:
**  [[metadir:gs-secdb-symbols-dev]], instead of using the URL keyword.
**
**  You can link to Issue Tracker pages using <tt>[[IS:6012-4710]]</tt> which renders as [[IS:6012-4710]].
**  You can provide your own text to describe the issue using <tt>[[IS:6012-4710|My Issue]]</tt> which
**  renders as [[IS:6012-4710|My Issue]].
**  If you want the subject line of the issue to show up in your SLAM, use <tt>[[Issue:6012-4710]]</tt> which
**  renders as [[Issue:6012-4710]]. Links with bad issue ids show up as [[Issue:bogus-id-6012-4710]].
**
**  Similarly, you can refer to Jira tickets using <tt>[[JI:SLANG-1]]</tt> which renders as [[JI:SLANG-1]].
**  You can put the full ticket title in the link by using <tt>[[Jira:SLANG-1]]</tt> which renders as [[Jira:SLANG-1]].
**  Bad issue links look like [[JI:BAAAAD-1]].
**
**  You can also specify a FAQ using the \[\[ syntax, with
**  the object name as specified in the Dev Db as your target. Again
**  information with a | will be the text. If there is no text we will
**  use the name of the FAQ. For example, you can consult the following
**  [[FAQE Doctor, it hurts I dS7N  0|Sagely advice]].
**
**  Furthermore, you can now specify a RegTest entry using that same \[\[
**  syntax. The rest is quite similar to FAQ entries described above. The
**  end result is [[RegTE SLAM Functions 0|this]]. RegTGs support the same
**  syntax too, for example [[RegTG Core.CodeQual.SLAM]].
**
**  A script review request can be linked by simply putting its full name in the square brackets:
**  \[\[Review 20101014 6010-97983\]\] which renders as [[Review 20101014 6010-97983]]
**
**  A ProcMon job (or job name pattern) can be linked by prefixing with "Proc:"
**  \[\[Proc:dbmaint/check/DeadHostsUpdate\]\] which renders as [[Proc:dbmaint/check/DeadHostsUpdate]].
**  You can specify a particular master with
**  \[\[Proc:secdba:sync/conflict/conflict~userdbs\]\] which renders as [[Proc:secdba:sync/conflict/conflict~userdbs]].
**
**  Yet another set of keywords allow you to link to projects in [http://projito.site.gs.com/projito Projito].
**  Use <tt>[[Proj:60207730]]</tt> show project name as label and link to the Projito web page
**  for this project, rendered as [[Proj:60207730]]. The syntax <tt>[[Projito:60207730]]</tt> is also supported.
**  Links with bad project ids show up as [[Proj:bogus-id-60124710]].
****************************************************************/
SLAM Example::Robert = Func(
    Date( Stairway ), // If there's a diddle on your hedge node
    Double( To Heaven ) // don't be alarmed now
)
Returns( Curve() )
{
    Return( Curve( [ Stairway, To Heaven ] ) );
};

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: SLAM Example::Jimmy
**
**  If you wish to specify an equation, you can specify inline
**  by surrounding in \<math\>'s. In a paragraph of their own
**  this markup will be a centred equation, otherwise it will
**  flow inline (but probably not have the baseline be correct.
**  Please, don't ask). We use '''LaTeX''' as our equation processor,
**  so you can use all the LaTeX equation markup, as described
**  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Formula in this wikipedia article].
**
**  For example, if I said our ansatz for the spacetime metric is
**  <math>ds^2 = - dt^2 + dr^2 + \left[ r - \lambda f(r) \right]^2d\Omega^2 , \quad 0 \leq \lambda f < r</math>
**  then you might want to talk about a Gaussian bowl:
**
**  <math>f(r) = f_{\rm G} = e^{-(r-r_0)^2/\sigma^2}</math>
**
**  Or chat around the Fermi bowl
**
**  <math>f(r) = f_{\rm F} = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\sigma (r-r_0)}}.</math>
**
**  If you wonder why we'd discuss that, you can see a full treatment
**  [http://lanl.arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9809004 in this really boring paper].
**  You can see the extent of the boringness in this table.
**
**  <table>
**    <tr>
**      <th>Activity</th><th>Boringness</th><th>Danger</th>
**    </tr>
**    <tr>
**      <td>Sleeping</td><td>High</td><td>Generally low, but exceptions summarized in this embedded table:
**
**  But first a numbered list:
**
**  # First
**  # Second
**
**        <table>
**          <tr><th>Location</th><th>Specific Danger Level</th></tr>
**          <tr><td>Bed</td>    <td>Low</td></tr>
**          <tr><td>Sea bed</td><td>High, unless crustacean</td></tr>
**          <tr><td>Overtaking lane of Peripherique</td><td>Off the scale</td></tr>
**        </table>
**      And here is a comment after the table. And another numbered list:
**
**  # Premiere
**  # Second
**      </td>
**    </tr>
**    <tr>
**      <td>Bungee Jumping</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td>
**    </tr>
**    <tr>
**      <td>PhD in General Relativity</td><td>
**      High. In fact <math>G_{\mu\nu} = 8 \pi T_{\mu\nu}</math>
**     </td>
**        <td>
**  This is the paragraph content
**
**  # Extreme
**  # High
**  # Scary
**        </td>
**    </tr>
**    <tr>
**      <td>Listening to the Rolling Stones</td>
**      <td>Extremely High</td>
**      <td>
**  [[Image:black and blue.jpg]]
**      </td>
**    </tr>
**  </table>
****************************************************************/

SLAM Example::Jimmy = Func(
    Structure( Kashmir ) // traveller of both time and space, to be where I have been
)
Returns( Array() )
{
    Return( Kashmir.Keys() );
};

// == Metallica ==

/****************************************************************
**  Routine: SLAM Example::Metallica
**
**  Much like the equation tag, you can also specify in [http://www.graphviz.org/pdf/dotguide.pdf DOT format]
**  an inline graph.  When embedded in scripts, it should only be limited to
**  graphs that can be human readable when reading the source code.  More complex graphs
**  should be removed to embedded SSP generated images.  However, in FAQ entries, this allows you to
**  specify relatively complex relationship diagrams quickly and easily in a standard format.
**
**  An example is in order:
**
**  <graph>
**      digraph G {
**          rankdir=LR
**          twisting [shape=box]
**          turning  [shape=box]
**          twisting -> turning
**          turning -> TheNever [label="through"]
**      }
**  </graph>
**
**  ><graph>
**  >    digraph G {
**  >        twisting [shape=box]
**  >        turning  [shape=box]
**  >        twisting -> turning
**  >        turning -> TheNever [label="through"]
**  >    }
**  ></graph>
**
**  To make things exciting, you can also include by reference the source of a function, TypeDefineStreamable'd typed structure method,
**  or VT method.  Typically inline code should be used in FAQ's or issues since function documentation should be descriptive
**  of the functions behaviour without referring to other functions code body.
**
**  Also, note that if functions should change, documentation might grow stale since the inline tag dynamically pulls the information
**  out of the source database when the SLAM document is generated.  Therefore the relevance of your references to it may no longer
